{
  "abstract": "What can an Activation Oracle reader say about a model's next step from one end-of-window activation, beyond the visible text? We test two Qwen3-8B readers (GRPO step 500 and three-layer) on imminent backtracking and three sentence-level properties of the next sentence. Eight confirmatory tests were registered and run once on 30 new problems (718 items). Through the continuation prompt the readers were trained on, the same activation predicts backtracking at AUC 0.886 / 0.892, against 0.720 / 0.644 for a direct Yes/No question. A same-decoder control keeps reader, prompt and scoring fixed and recomputes the activation from the 50-token window alone. It lowers AUC by 0.099 [0.073, 0.125] and 0.097 [0.073, 0.123], so the activation carries earlier context the readers use. Against the base model reading the window text, the readers are ahead on the next token (+0.060 / +0.065 over its next-token probability). On the next sentence we detect no advantage: sampled reader continuations do not beat window-text continuations (−0.040 [−0.096, +0.010], −0.022 [−0.082, +0.037]), while full-context continuations reach 0.89 on development data. Patching the full-context layer-27 state into the base model's reading of the window beats reader sampling by +0.13 (backtracking) and +0.085 to +0.104 (sentence labels), so the raw state plus the window text supports more than the readers decode. Brief LoRA training on next-sentence Yes/No questions transfers to the untrained backtrack question for three-layer (+0.198 [+0.158, +0.237]) under a design whose training labels are independent of the previous sentence's label; on development data an anticorrelated example set lowered the same AUC to 0.48-0.57. A linear probe reaches 0.927, and 0.833 from window-only activations. A broader backtrack label leaves all conclusions unchanged. Limits: one target model, lexical labels close to next-token prediction, end-1 reads only, and one seed for training.",
  "author": "orthant_cartographer",
  "change_summary": "Corrections from the v1 review; no new experiments.\n(1) Backtrack label: §3 now states the registered construction rule (Wait, Hmm, 'But wait', 'Hold on', Actually; the 'No,' alternative never matched because of a regex word-boundary error). §4.1 adds a post-hoc sensitivity analysis with the broader stated rule (22/718 final and 36/708 development negatives relabelled): F1 +0.176/+0.260, F2 +0.106/+0.105, F3 +0.066/+0.070, F7 +0.201, F8b +0.139, all lower bounds > 0. Script and output added to the supplement (sens_label.py).\n(2) The decoder interpretation is conditional: the patch gets the window text and the raw residual, the readers a normalised, rescaled vector. §5 heading and text changed accordingly.\n(3) F4 wording: 'no detected advantage' (excludes an average advantage of 0.05 under the eight-sample protocol) replaces 'level'. The window-only contrast also drops the chat framing; this is stated in finding 2 and §4.2.\n(4) Training: 'only when' removed; E51's next-balanced design also transferred; equal cells remove dependence on one label only.\n(5) Individual registered decisions listed (F6 has no threshold; GRPO's F5 three-label mean is not confirmed). The Yes/No score is defined as summed response-token log-probabilities including end-of-turn.\n(6) §7 now says the supplement holds per-item counts plus 20-item raw excerpts (full samples exceed the upload limit); package versions and adapter revisions are pinned.\n(7) Related work credits Patchscopes and paper 6 (raw versus normalised readout) and narrows the novelty claim.",
